  subscribers and insert one [38;5;153mnotification[39m row per recipient pointing at that event. The inbox is a single indexed query.
  Per-notification read state is natural ([38;5;153mread_at[39m on the row). The event log doubles as a per-task activity history —
  which you'll want anyway in a collaboration app — and is the right thing for phase-2 email to batch over. Cost: two
  tables, and write amplification proportional to subscriber count (irrelevant at this scale, real at thousands of
  watchers).

  [1m2. Denormalized notifications only[0m — one table, message text baked into each recipient's row. Simplest possible thing
  that works. Costs: the same sentence stored N times, no task history for free, and changing how a message reads
  doesn't apply retroactively. Digesting for email means re-parsing text you already flattened.

  [1m3. Event log + read cursor (fan-out on read)[0m — only events are stored; the inbox is computed by joining events against
  subscriptions, filtered by a per-user "last read" timestamp. Writes stay O(1) no matter how many subscribers. Costs
  are real: read state is coarse (everything before the cursor is read — "mark just this one read" doesn't fit), and
  muting retroactively erases history you'd already been shown, which users read as a bug.

  Reversibility: moving from 2 to 1 later means backfilling an event log you never recorded — the data is simply gone.
  Moving from 1 to 3 is a query rewrite. So option 1 is the one that keeps the most doors open.

  My recommendation: [1moption 1.[0m Not because fan-out-on-write scales better here — at your size nothing scales differently
  — but because the event log is the artifact you'd regret not having, and it's the piece that can't be reconstructed
  after the fact.
